  <h1>Why Does Anything Exist?</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">Not a proposed earlier particle or instant, but a conditional chain: two chosen
    boundary meanings, Euclidean closure, a quarter-turn in complex phase, and a conjectured route to matter.</p>
</section>

{% include "partials/editorial-guide.njk" %}

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Begin Here</p>
    <h2 id="t-two-boundary-conditions">Two Boundary Conditions</h2>
    <p class="lede">TTI assigns two ontological roles to the familiar numbers zero and one: an open limit
      toward absence and a completed boundary of unity. Those roles are the framework's premises, not
      properties proved by arithmetic.</p>

    <p>For example, the positive sequence \(1,\tfrac12,\tfrac14,\ldots\) converges to zero without any
      finite term equalling zero. Zero itself is still an ordinary real number and can plainly be the value
      of a quantity; it is the particular asymptotic approach that never arrives. TTI turns that approach
      into its &ldquo;smallward&rdquo; image. It similarly uses one as the endpoint of a normalised interval and
      then interprets completion as the boundary of a distinguishable thing. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>

    <p>The exact result between the endpoints is cardinal. The real numbers in the interval \((0,1)\) are
      uncountable, whereas the natural numbers \(1,2,3,\ldots\) are countable.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> The interval therefore has a strictly larger cardinality; calling it
      &ldquo;denser&rdquo; would risk confusing cardinality with the separate topological idea of density. TTI's
      further move is to interpret the cardinal asymmetry as a foundational tilt and, later, as a possible
      orientation of time. Cantor's theorem does not supply that temporal interpretation.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \left|\mathbb{R}\cap(0,1)\right|=2^{\aleph_0}>\aleph_0=\left|\mathbb{N}\right|
    \]</p>

    <p class="muted small">Grading: <span class="sci">&#9670; established mathematics or physics</span>
      &middot; <span class="hist">&#9670; history and scholarship</span> &middot;
      <span class="spec">&#9670; the framework's own argument, contested</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; vision, stated as vision</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">A new conjecture, not part of Cantor&rsquo;s proof</p>
    <h2 id="t-temporal-antipodes">The Two Infinities May Occupy Opposite Ends of Time</h2>
    <p>The framework now asks whether the smallward infinity near zero and the largeward infinity of
      unbounded magnitude are not merely asymmetric in scale, but <strong>temporal antipodes</strong>. On that
      reading they cannot fully synchronise: a common clock would require a shared finite instant and a lawful
      comparison channel, while the proposed boundary states sit at opposite asymptotic ends.</p>
    <p>This does not follow from cardinal arithmetic, and &ldquo;smallward&rdquo; does not mean smaller in
      cardinality. Finite projections may cross, recur or nearly align without making their two origins
      simultaneous. The standalone essay defines synchronisation, compactifies the time coordinate and states
      the assumptions under which <em>cannot</em> is justified. Read
      <a href="/temporal-antipodes/">Temporal Antipodes</a>. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">1.2 &mdash; The Constant of Closure</p>
    <h2 id="t-pi">π Is Not Found Inside the Universe. It Is the Condition of There Being One.</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">Ask how zero and one are joined. Draw the path straight and it never closes: it
      runs on forever, resolving nothing, producing nothing finite that could stand and stay.</p>
  </div>

