  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame">
      <p><strong>The compressed claim now has a full mathematical account.</strong> The standalone note
        <a href="/pi-from-zero-and-one/">Why π Appears Between Zero and One</a> proves
        \(B(\tfrac12,\tfrac12)=\int_0^1[x(1-x)]^{-1/2}dx=\pi\), opens that integral into a unit
        semicircle, and checks it against the Gregory&ndash;Leibniz and Basel theorems. It also states the
        missing premise: the result requires a Euclidean metric and circular closure; 0 and 1 do not produce
        π by bare arithmetic. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
