<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">1.3 &mdash; The Split That Opens a Dimension</p>
    <h2 id="t-the-i-split">A Quarter-Turn Exposes the Complex Direction</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame">
      <p>Euler's formula describes rotation in a complex plane that already contains the imaginary unit.
        A half-turn carries one to negative one; a quarter-turn carries one to i:
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        e^{i\pi}+1=0
        \qquad	ext{and}\qquad
        e^{i\pi/2}=i
      \]</p>
      <p>This equation does not manufacture i from π; i is part of the formula used to define the rotation.
        What it establishes is an exact relation among exponential growth, circular phase and a perpendicular
        axis. TTI then makes a separate interpretive move: it reads the quarter-turn as a model for dimensional
        opening. That reading may be illuminating, but it is not another theorem hidden inside Euler's
        identity. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p>The complex plane is not a decorative afterthought in the standard formulation of quantum
        mechanics: states live in complex Hilbert spaces and relative phase changes measurable interference.
        A prominent 2021 network experiment separated standard complex quantum theory from a specifically
        defined real-amplitude rival. The conclusion is narrower than this page previously implied. Later work
        showed that broader real-arithmetic formulations can reproduce the same predictions by changing how
        composite systems are represented. The safe claim is therefore that complex structure is
        <em>load-bearing in the standard formalism</em>, not that experiment has proved the symbol i to be an
        irreducible substance of nature. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">A later extension, not a result of Chapter 1</p>
    <h3 id="t-negative-interval">What the Interval \((-1,0)\) Does Not Yet Mean</h3>
    <p>Euler&rsquo;s half-turn places \(-1\) on the real line, and the interval \((-1,0)\) is mathematically as
      well defined as \((0,1)\). Nothing in that fact makes it an earlier universe, a hidden physical region
      or a store of transferable matter. Negative coordinate, negative energy and reversed orientation are
      different claims.</p>
    <p>The later <a href="/little-red-dots/">little-red-dots conjecture</a> asks whether this interval could
      parameterise an orientation-reversed residue sector. Its
      <a href="/little-red-dots/research-proposal/">Zero-Seam research proposal</a> accepts the resulting
      burden: define the state space, derive a crossing map, choose what is conserved and make a prediction
      ordinary astrophysics does not already supply. Until then, the physical reading is chosen rather than
      earned. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">1.4 &mdash; The Proposed Chain of Dependence</p>
    <h2 id="t-chain">Six Links — and Where Necessity Ends</h2>
  </div>
  <ul class="timeline">
    <li><strong>Zero and one exist as boundary conditions.</strong> Not as quantities but as the limits of
      measurability. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
    <li><strong>Their relation cannot be straight.</strong> A straight relation never closes, and an open
      relation produces nothing finite. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
    <li><strong>Normalised Euclidean circular closure introduces π.</strong> The
      <a href="/pi-from-zero-and-one/">unit-interval beta integral</a> states the exact result; choosing that
      geometry as a condition on reality remains speculative. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span>
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
    <li><strong>A quarter-turn in the complex plane carries 1 to i.</strong> Euler's formula states that
      relation exactly; interpreting it as dimensional creation is TTI's additional move.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
    <li><strong>The real and imaginary axes span the complex plane.</strong> This is established mathematics;
      the claim that the second axis is physically generated by closure is not.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
    <li><strong>The complex plane houses the wavefunction, and the wavefunction is matter.</strong>
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
  </ul>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">1.5</p>
    <h2 id="t-not-fragile">Why Existence Is Not Fragile</h2>
    <p>If existence rested on a cause, one could always ask what caused the cause, and the question would
      recede forever. On this reading nothing is holding existence up, because there is nothing it could
      fall from. Zero and one are not events that happened; they are conditions that cannot fail to obtain.
      The closure between them is not an achievement but a consequence. <strong>Nothing is geometrically
      impossible</strong> &mdash; and so something is unavoidable.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">The Ledger for This Chapter</p>
    <h2 id="t-ledger">What Survives, and What Does Not</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Earned</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Cantor's result: the unit interval is uncountable, the integers are not.
          <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
        <li>\(e^{i\pi/2}=i\), and the whole of Euler's identity. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
        <li>Standard quantum theory uses complex amplitudes, and experiments rule out particular
          real-amplitude foil theories under their stated composition assumptions. Whether complex numbers
          are uniquely fundamental rather than representational remains debated. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Chosen, and openly so</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>That zero and one should be read as <em>boundary conditions on existence</em> rather than as
          numbers. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
        <li>That &ldquo;the relation must close&rdquo; is a requirement on reality rather than on a
          diagram. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
        <li>That the wavefunction <em>is</em> matter, rather than describing it &mdash; a position in the
          philosophy of quantum mechanics, not a result. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="frame card">
    <h3>Refused</h3>
    <p>Any claim that this derives the Standard Model, predicts a constant, or replaces a field equation.
      It does not. It is an argument about why there is a stage &mdash; and it is judged on whether that
      argument holds, not on physics it never attempted.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow">
  <dl class="glossary">
    <dt>Boundary condition</dt>
    <dd>A limit that constrains a system without being a member of it. Zero and one are used here in that
      sense, as the floor and the edge of measurability.</dd>
    <dt>Closure</dt>
    <dd>The property of a path that returns to itself. In this framework, the minimum requirement for
      anything finite to persist.</dd>
    <dt>The i-split</dt>
    <dd>The half-turn \(e^{i\pi/2}=i\), read as the creation of a perpendicular dimension rather than
      as a rotation within an existing one.</dd>
    <dt>The denser infinity</dt>
    <dd>The uncountable infinity inside (0, 1), contrasted with the countable infinity of the integers.</dd>
  </dl>
</section>

