<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; the chain, read backwards</p>
    <h2 id="t-irreversible">Why the Chain Will Not Run the Other Way</h2>
    <p>A chain of necessity is only worth the name if it is directed. Six links that could be traversed in
      either order would be a list, not a derivation. So it is worth stating plainly what forbids the
      return journey at each joint.</p>
    <p>From α to π the step is <em>closure</em>: an interval acquires a second side. Closure destroys
      information about where on the interval you began, which is exactly why it cannot be undone &mdash;
      a circle does not remember its cut. From π to π/2 the step is <em>splitting</em>: one rotation becomes
      two independent half-rotations, and the imaginary axis appears. Splitting is not a loss but a
      promotion, and promotions are not reversible either; you cannot unsplit a plane back into a line
      without choosing which direction to discard, and nothing in the geometry makes that choice. From π/2
      to i the step is <em>orientation</em>, and orientation is the one thing a half-twist takes away
      permanently. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="lede">Each link forgets something. That is what makes the chain a direction rather than a
      sequence, and it is the earliest appearance in this work of the arrow that
      <a href="/seams/#t-arrow">Part III &mdash; Seams and Return</a> has to account for.</p>
    <p class="muted">The honest reservation: &ldquo;cannot be undone&rdquo; is doing heavy lifting across
      three different senses &mdash; information loss, dimensional promotion, and loss of orientability.
      They are genuinely different kinds of irreversibility, and a finished version of this argument would
      prove that each one implies a thermodynamic direction rather than merely resembling it. It does
      not.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; from Chapter 23, <em>A Number Made of Itself</em></p>
    <h2 id="t-pi-made">What It Means to Say π Is Not Found Inside the Universe</h2>
    <p>The claim at the head of this chapter concerns an order of dependence, but that order must be stated
      carefully. Parameters such as the fine-structure constant and particle-mass ratios are learned from
      observation. Their values in our universe are empirical; claims about how they would vary in another
      universe are model-dependent counterfactuals, not measurements.</p>
    <p>π is different because it is fixed by mathematical definitions. In Euclidean geometry it gives the
      circumference-to-diameter ratio of every circle and appears in many equivalent theorems without being
      remeasured each time. That does <em>not</em> make π the condition for every closed curve: curved spaces
      and non-circular loops also close, and their geometry must be specified. TTI's narrower proposal is to
      choose Euclidean circular closure as its foundational model and ask what follows.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>This is why the framework keeps returning to π rather than to an empirically fitted parameter. A
      theorem can be reasoned through once its premises are declared. Applying its geometry to the universe
      is still a modelling choice, and observation must decide whether that choice describes nature.
      <a href="/essays/#t-number">The accompanying essay</a> puts the poetic intuition more briefly: the one
      number the universe did not have to go and find.</p>
    <p class="muted">What is refused here: the slide from &ldquo;π is necessary within the chosen Euclidean
      construction&rdquo; to &ldquo;π therefore caused the physical universe&rdquo;. Logical consequence is not agency,
      and mathematical necessity inside a model is not evidence that nature selected that model.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">An accessible route through the claim</p>
    <h2 id="t-three-moves">Three Moves That Must Not Be Blurred Together</h2>
    <p>The argument becomes easier to assess when it is read as three separate moves. First comes a
      mathematical fact: 0 and 1 can bound a normalised interval, and Euclidean closure introduces π. Second
      comes a modelling choice: TTI treats those endpoints as names for absence and completed unity. Third
      comes an interpretation: a world may be understood as the continuing passage between those limits.
      Only the first move is a theorem. The second and third are the framework being proposed.</p>
    <p>A simple picture helps. Draw a line from 0 to 1 on paper. The marks at its ends do not create a circle.
      If the line is required to return smoothly, however, geometry must say what “return” means. In a flat
      Euclidean plane, the familiar answer is circular closure, and π enters every honest measurement of that
      closure. This is why the next essay derives π from an integral on the interval instead of asserting that
      the digits of 0 and 1 somehow contain it.</p>
    <p>The same distinction governs the larger story. A map can constrain a journey without pushing the
      traveller. Likewise, a mathematical condition can restrict possible structures without acting as a
      physical cause. TTI uses 0, 1 and π as a map of dependence: boundaries first, closure second, physical
      interpretation later. A field equation, observation or experiment is still needed before the map earns
      authority over nature.</p>
    <p class="lede">The shortest fair summary is therefore conditional: <em>if</em> existence is modelled as
      a bounded relation that must close, <em>and if</em> the closure is Euclidean and circular, then π is not
      an ornamental number added afterwards. It is required by the construction.</p>
    <p>This formulation also shows where disagreement belongs. A reader may reject the ontological meanings
      assigned to 0 and 1, choose a non-Euclidean geometry, or deny that existence needs global closure. Any
      of those objections challenges a declared premise. What would be unfair is to call the beta integral
      false because the cosmology is speculative, or to call the cosmology proved because the integral is
      exact. The evidence ledger keeps those two errors apart.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- expand:foundations:end -->

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">Closing Stance</p>
    <p>The chapter does not claim that no other explanation of existence could work. It claims that this
      one requires no ingredients beyond two numbers and the constant that joins them &mdash; and that a
      theory asking for less is hard to imagine. Press it where it is weakest: at the word
      <em>must</em>.</p>
    <p class="muted small">Next in <a href="/pi-from-zero-and-one/">Part I &mdash; First Principles: π from 0 and 1</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

